it takes a long time to make a movie  even if a production does completely  stay on schedule so if a scene proves to  be more complicated than others things  can get out of control relatively  quickly every now and again you'll hear  of massive delays and setbacks that  emerge mid chute whether it's due to  onset injuries or a centerpiece action  scene eating up huge amounts of extra  time or perhaps a demanding director who  just wants several dozen takes to have  some choices in the editing room  unforeseen circumstances surrounding a  scene can make a movies production  process go right off the rails here are  some movie scenes that ended up being  unexpectedly massive undertakings for  the cast and crew spidey-senses before  there was the marvel cinematic universe  Peter Parker swung on two screens in  director Sam Raimi's 2002 blockbuster  spider-man the film helped usher in the  new superhero movie Renaissance since  Hollywood was still trying to figure out  what comic-book movie audiences really  wanted to see  similarly influx was the state of  special-effects though the digital  revolution rolled out through the 90s  CGI was still undergoing the occasional  growing pains at the turn of the  millennium  many of Spidey stunts would be achieved  digitally but they don't exactly hold up  when compared to what the webhead gets  up to in his newer adventures Raimi has  often spun his fascination with sleight  of hand magic into low-budget camera  tricks but he was determined to achieve  Peter Parker spidey sense for real in  the film for a scene in which his newly  enhanced agility allows Peter to rescue  Mary Jane from a slip and catch her  entire airborne lunch neatly on a tray  no computer assistance was used Maguire  managed the trick himself with some  adhesive and props dropped strategically  from above of course that doesn't mean  it wasn't difficult the shot took 156  takes over a 16 hour shooting day true  abyss the true story of the RMS Titanic  is often seen as warning about mankind's  hubris the massive vessel was built to  break records and demonstrate English  superiority while catering to the  wealthiest people and it was woefully  unprepared for the disaster that ensued  when it struck an iceberg during  production James Cameron's 1997 film  Titanic seemed destined to fall victim  to ambition as well  Cameron intended to set an original love  story against the backdrop of a  painstaking recreation of the original  ship and it's sinking elaborate sets  difficult condition  and Cameron's exacting perfectionism  saddled the project with a constantly  bloating budget in a schedule that  spiraled out of control as it became the  most expensive movie ever made at the  time surprisingly while the intricate  ship sets and harrowing sinking scenes  provided Titanic's most dazzling moments  the most difficult seems to shoot  involve no effects or recreations at all  in fact it was Cameron himself who  embarked on a dozen expeditions to the  real shipwreck more than two miles below  the ocean's surface over the course of  two years to research material for the  film that was struck just by the the  surreal nature of the fact that I was  sitting here on this deck on these  lengthy and often dangerous dives  Cameron shot extensive footage of the  Titanic's present state for use in the  movies opening scenes the risks paid off  as the film became an unprecedented hit  hailed for its grandeur and spectacle  and earning more than its due at the box  office but it's hard to imagine anyone  else besides James Cameron who'd commit  to such an arduous undertaking just to  get a few authentic shots Spielberg's  sinking not only did Steven Spielberg's  jaws to find the modern summer  blockbuster it also demonstrated how to  create tension by withholding the reveal  of what the audience is afraid of this  wasn't entirely intentional as the  mechanical sharks frequent breakdowns  for Spielberg to show the beasts much  less than he originally planned the  young director was forced to develop  clever ways to suggest the man-eaters  presence without the use of the troubled  robot one of these quick fixes  ironically led to the most disastrous  day of an already beleaguered production  the heroic fisherman harpooned the shark  with the series of floating barrels that  signal its presence beneath the surface  for a scene in which the ominous  floaters circle the boat known as the  Orca the film's effects technicians  affixed a pulley system that controlled  their movement to the bottom of the hull  unfortunately the planks of the Orca  weren't strong enough to support this  rig and a quick yank on the barrels  ropes tore a hole in the boat's hull it  began to sink rapidly sending the crew  scrambling to rescue the cast equipment  and an entire day's footage from the sea  although this and several other  disasters push the shooting schedule  from the planned 55 days to a whopping  final tally of 159 days the Orca was  repaired the footage was saved and jaws  went on to reshape movie history  Disney's near-disaster Fantasia  the third animated feature from Walt  Disney Studios is a marvel on every  level particularly in the context of its  1940 production it may have been  underappreciated at the time but the  achievement of Fantasia's marriage of  technical innovation with artistic  ambition is hard to deny for the grand  finale of their concert feature Disney  and his artists envisioned a climactic  clash between the sacred and profane  pairing Modest Mussorgsky 'z Night on  Bald Mountain with Franz Schubert's Ave  Maria and it was quite an undertaking  the visuals for Ave Maria called for the  longest single shot in the history of  animation at the time as the camera  followed a procession through a densely  layered forest and into a Cathedral of  trees this meant that a lineup of  background paintings had to be set up in  to end across the length of a huge  soundstage the scene was completed once  only to have the horrified crew realized  they had put the wrong lens on the  camera a second attempt was foiled by an  earthquake all the while the scene was  frequently halted so that the soundstage  could be used for Walt's weekly  badminton game needless to say the final  shot took way longer than anyone  expected and in fact it was only  achieved in just enough time for the  film reels to be flown to New York mere  hours before Fantasia's premiere now  that's a close call
 

